<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yung Wing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rong Hong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yung Wing was a Chinese-American diplomat and businessman who in 1854 became the first recorded Chinese graduate of an American university, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale College</a>. In 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> sent him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to buy machinery, which went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. He persuaded the Qing government to send young students abroad and organised the Chinese Educational Mission, which brought 120 of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> from 1872 until it was disbanded in 1881. A naturalised American citizen from 1852, he lost that status under the Chinese Exclusion Act.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yung-wing.jpg" alt="Yung Wing" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Hsu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yung_Wing_Frontispiece_My_Life_in_China_and_America_1909_FRD_4814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
